# Dynamic pathfinding on the NeoGeo

One of the core mechanics I wanted to experiment with for my little NeoGeo tower defense prototype was dynamic pathfinding. Not just computing a path once, but allowing the player to change the map while enemies are already moving.

The current implementation is intentionally simple. The map is stored as a regular grid where every cell can represent a street, grass, or a blocking obstacle. Whenever the terrain changes, the complete path map is recalculated.

The algorithm itself is a flood fill (or Dijkstra-style distance map). Starting from the goal, the routine goes through all reachable cells. Streets currently have a very low movement cost, grass is more expensive (20 times more than street), and blocked cells simply stop the path. The result is a map where every reachable tile contains the cost of reaching the goal.

The nice thing about this representation is that moving an enemy becomes almost trivial. Every "walker" simply inspects the four neighbouring cells (up, down, left and right) and moves towards the one with the lowest value. Since the values always decrease towards the goal, the enemy eventually finds its way there without ever having to compute an individual path.

The video below demonstrates the current state. Using the controller I can place or remove streets, paint grass, or insert blocking obstacles. Every modification immediately causes the distance map to be recomputed, and the walker automatically adapt to the new shortest route.

=> https://youtu.be/qq8ABav1Y5A YouTube Neo Geo Dev #3 Pathfinding

At this stage movement is still tile-by-tile, so the walker runs across the map much faster than they eventually will. That is actually quite convenient while testing since it allows me to verify that path updates work correctly without having to wait for slow-moving units. Pixel-accurate movement has already been implemented in a newer version, but this earlier prototype made debugging the pathfinding itself much easier.

Although there are many possible pathfinding algorithms, I particularly like this approach for a tower defense game. The expensive work happens only when the map changes, while the enemies themselves merely compare four neighbouring values every update. That makes the movement logic extremely small and deterministic—something that feels very much at home on a 1990 arcade machine.
And the looking-up-neighbours-code works quite well on the NeoGeo's m68k:

``` m68k
    ; neighbour: up
	lea -2(a0),a2
	lea -2(a1),a3
	bsr.s TryRelax

	; neighbour: down
	lea 2(a0),a2
	lea 2(a1),a3
	bsr.s TryRelax

	; neighbour: left
	lea -ROWBYTES(a0),a2
	lea -ROWBYTES(a1),a3
	bsr.s TryRelax

	; neighbour: right
	lea ROWBYTES(a0),a2
	lea ROWBYTES(a1),a3
	bsr.s TryRelax
```

with a0 and a1 pointing into the pathmap and terrainbuffer.
a2/a3 are then evaluated

